-- The schema's rationale, as COMMENT ON statements, under the names the
-- baseline's tables and columns carry. cmd/schemabaseline applies this file
-- before it dumps, so the baseline carries every comment and a database born
-- on it answers obj_description / col_description.
--
-- Carried from the retired baseline DDL (store/schema/postgres_ddl.go at
-- v0.16.0) and from the table notes of the DDL-totality test of that release.
-- What those comments said about the replay itself (which statement ran ahead
-- of which migration, which index had to wait for which version) described a
-- boot path that no longer exists and is not carried. One statement per line:
-- a comment's text has no line breaks.

COMMENT ON COLUMN nodes.display_name IS 'The person-readable label (v144). The node''s own name is the fleet''s identity: scenesync matches the fleet''s location set by name and deletes a physical node the scene does not carry, so the cosmetic rename rides a column of its own; empty renders the name.';

COMMENT ON COLUMN bin_types.length_in IS 'The third dimension (v90). The table carried width and height only, which left a code like 45x58x32 holding its own length in the string, where no query could reach it. Width x length is a type''s footprint: a label and a check, never a decision. What a cart may be stamped as is its carrier family (v172); a slot''s fit is type identity everywhere.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN bin_types.totes_per_layer IS 'How many totes one layer of this carrier holds (v161). NULL: not a tote carrier, or not said.';

COMMENT ON COLUMN payloads.tote_layers IS 'The layers of totes a full cart stands (v161). NULL is unknown.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN payloads.uop_per_tote IS 'The UOP (in the capacity''s unit: pieces, for a payload of one part) that fill one tote (v161). NULL is unknown.';

COMMENT ON TABLE payload_containment IS 'The per-payload quality containment flag. A quality alert on a part sets active, and every FG-bound delivery of that payload diverts to its claim''s containment destination instead of the FG drop zone. The audit columns are written by the store on every set and clear: containment nobody can attribute is containment nobody can defend. One row per payload code; the row persists after deactivation so the history (who, when, why) survives the alert clearing.';

COMMENT ON TABLE load_sequences IS 'The advanced-load-sequence registry. One row per sequence name; task_names is a JSON array of binTask names in execution order. Editable data (a plant names its RDS-side binTask keys differently); seeded by v50 with Child cart interlock. A payload selects a sequence by name via payloads.advanced_load_sequence.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN load_sequences.lift_at IS 'The index in task_names of the name that lifts (v179).';

COMMENT ON COLUMN bins.quality_hold IS 'Quality containment: the per-bin hold marker an operator''s Send to Quality Hold sets. A held bin is not re-sourceable and not re-releasable to FG while it waits for (or sits in) containment. Default false is every other bin.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN bins.lifted_at IS 'When a streamed carrier''s generation ended at a lift (v151): set with the bump in the lift''s UPDATE and cleared at the arrival. A late window starting at or after it is not the lifted carrier''s (refused as after_lift).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN bins.totes_on_cart IS 'The totes standing on the cart, filled or empty (v161). NULL is unknown; 0 is tops and bottoms. Every statement that empties a carrier writes NULL.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN bins.last_lift_at IS 'When a robot last lifted the bin (v162). Every lift writes it, in the lift''s own UPDATE; nothing clears it. The vacate hold''s floor.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN bins.lifted_from_node_id IS 'The node a streamed carrier was lifted from (v175), written with lifted_at. A late draw of the generation the lift ended is booked there.';
COMMENT ON SEQUENCE bin_placement_seq IS 'The placement stamp (v150): one value per placement Core records, taken in the arrival''s own UPDATE and carried on the landing. A seat refuses a landing whose stamp is not newer than the one it last bound, so a redelivered or overtaken landing binds nothing. A sequence, not a clock, because a clock can step.';

COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.origin_id IS 'The demand this order was created to serve. See origin_class.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.origin_class IS 'Whether the order should have had a demand. It is what makes origin_id IS NULL answerable: without it that predicate selects every consume-side order, every opportunistic stage and every admin action, with the actual lost origins buried in there. Only orphan is a finding.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.open_for_children IS 'Sealedness, on the compound parent: sealed = NOT open_for_children. Sealed is the concept''s name everywhere else (SealDigGroup), and this is the column that carries it. Named for the exception so the safe state is the zero value in both languages: false here and false for a bare orders.Order literal in Go, and both mean sealed. Only a compound parent means anything by it: a reshuffle that may still gain children is not finished when its current children are all terminal, and all children terminal is what AdvanceCompoundOrder''s success arm and AdvanceStuckReshuffleParents read as done. Born sealed by DEFAULT false; only SetCompoundOpen changes it.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.station_unaware IS 'The station named on the order has no row for it: it did not send the order and the order does not project there. Its station is sent nothing about it. Named for the exception; false is every order but a Core-born one that does not project (v145).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.projects_to IS 'The station a station-unaware order''s delivered and picked_up notices go to; blank is nobody. Unread for a station-known order (v146).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.plan IS 'The planner''s pick at birth, as JSON: the bin, its node and the bin row as the pick saw it. Acquiring reads it instead of picking again, and picks only when it no longer stands. Blank is no pick (v147).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.orphan_aged_at IS 'When an order was judged an orphan (v61). A timestamp rather than a fourth origin_class, so aging records when a judgement was made without overwriting what the judgement was.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.destination_resolved_at IS 'When the store-slot selector chose this order''s destination, at intake. Written only by admission (admit), only when resolveSyntheticDestination rewrote a group into a concrete slot; NULL means the destination was not chosen at intake (named concretely by the sender, or the group was full and planMove resolves it at dispatch). It exists for one reader, the burial shadow''s tripwire (service/burial_shadow.go), which has to tell a placement that skipped the guard from a guard consulted before the world moved, and so has to know when the guard looked: intake resolves before the order row exists, and the fleet commit can follow minutes later when the order queues behind capacity. Nullable, and the fallback is the old behaviour: an order without a stamp is judged against fleet commit.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.recovers_order_id IS 'The cancelled order whose bin this order returns (v140). Set only on a Core-minted return for a bin left on a robot''s deck by a cancel; NULL on every other order.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.named_bin_id IS 'The one bin a move is for, named by the station that sent it (a keep-staged spare''s return; v141). The bin hold and claim statements take that bin and no other, and only while it stands on the source node. NULL on every other order. No foreign key: a bin row deleted under a waiting move must read as the bin has left, not turn the move into one that names nothing.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.want IS 'The height an empty is wanted at, as the station stated it on its request (protocol.OrderRequest.Want; v166): blank none, a decimal for that many layers, tops_bottoms for tops and bottoms only. Every find of the order reads it.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.steps_done IS 'How many steps of the order''s plan the fleet has finished, by position (v167): the block subscriber advances it as each block finishes the plan''s next open step (an order with no stored plan counts its two implied steps). The route strip reads where the robot is from it.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.block_actions IS 'What each block Core sent the fleet for this order is, by block id: a plan action (pickup, dropoff, wait) or blank for a block of no step''s kind (v179). Written by the create and by each append; the block subscriber reads a finished block''s meaning from it. NULL on an order sent before v179, read by the old name rule for its whole life.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.version IS 'One counter for everything the order''s station is told (v181): bumped in the same statement as a status write, a robot write that names a new robot, and a change of wait the station is told. It rides the order.update, the reconcile snapshot and the projection, and the station applies only a newer one. Born 1, so a 0 on the wire is an older Core.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN orders.asked_vendor_order_id IS 'The vendor order id Core is asking the fleet to create, from the hand that takes the order''s holds until the fleet answers; blank when Core is not asking (v182). Core-only, never on the wire. An open ask is spoken for whatever the status says.';
COMMENT ON INDEX idx_orders_uuid IS 'Unique, and partial. Two orders sharing an edge_uuid has no story: GetByUUID breaks the tie with ORDER BY id DESC, so a duplicate would silently redirect every lookup, including the ownership check behind cancel and release, onto the newer row. Blank is excluded because a blank means no Edge asked for this, and several rows can honestly be blank at once. The restore- exemption it once carried (for the retired put-back subsystem''s synthetic parents) went at v89: UUID means UUID for every order, and compound children mint a real one.';
COMMENT ON INDEX idx_orders_origin_id IS 'The child lookup. cost_ratio is COUNT(*) WHERE origin_id = X, the central query of the demand surface. Partial because most orders are structurally originless and indexing their NULLs buys nothing.';

COMMENT ON TABLE order_history IS 'Every status transition an order made, in order. The canonical timing source: both endpoints of execution time come from here rather than mission_telemetry, because these rows are written by Core''s own state machine, on Core''s clock. code, actor and ref are the typed reason (v55); all three are NULL on rows written before v55 and on uncoded transitions.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN order_history.code IS 'protocol.TermCode on a terminal row, protocol.QueueCode on a to-queued row. Prefer it over parsing detail, which is prose.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN order_history.actor IS 'Who caused the transition.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN order_history.ref IS 'What the reason concerns, as JSONB: node, payload, peer. The reference is what makes a reason actionable: ref->>payload is the GROUP BY that turns no_source_bin into starvation by cause.';

COMMENT ON COLUMN payload_manifest.parts_per_cycle IS 'A ratio, not a count: how many of this part one assembly cycle consumes, an assembly consume setting. A bin''s count of its parts is derived at read time from bins.uop_remaining and stored nowhere; the rule is protocol.ManifestItem''s. It was called quantity until v99 renamed it; the value did not change, because it was always the ratio. Default 1: a line with no stated ratio is one per cycle.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN payload_manifest.part_number IS 'Names a part, and has since it was created. Its values at the plants were cat ids, because the entry form asked for one: that was a data correction (v107, v108), not a reason to rename the column after the mistake.';

COMMENT ON TABLE edge_registry IS 'The identity table. station_uid is what Core correlates all history to; display_name is the operator''s label; station_id is the transport routing selector (Address.Station), whose value is the station_uid. Core mints the uid rather than the Pi because a Core-issued token is re-issuable: replace the hardware for an existing station and hand the new Pi the existing uid, and history does not move because identity did not move. Not the BIGSERIAL id either: it is real and it is read (registry.List scans it), but it is allocated by an INSERT ... ON CONFLICT driven by an Edge-supplied string, a consequence of the name, not independent of it. bound_hostname and conflict_* are the duplicate-edge detector (v64); bound_instance, prev_instance and bound_at are the binding lease (v66), for the case the hostname detector is blind to: two Pis flashed from one SD image share a hostname. See registry.Register for why recurrence, an instance coming back after being displaced, is the signature a restarting single Pi can never produce. The conflict_* columns are the alarm''s persisted state, not a derived view: the question has this ever happened here has to be answerable in SQL months later.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN edge_registry.station_uid IS 'What Core correlates all history to. Core mints it at enrollment and it never changes. Opaque on purpose: the moment an identifier is legible it acquires an editor.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN edge_registry.display_name IS 'The operator''s label (PLANT / EDGE-2). Operator-owned, freely mutable, and it never crosses the wire as an identifier.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN edge_registry.station_id IS 'The transport routing selector, Address.Station. Its value is the station_uid; the column survives because every station-keyed row in this database already carries that string.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN edge_registry.claimed_at IS 'NULL until a human has said what this station is. It is not status: Register writes status = active on every register, so anything recorded there is erased by the next heartbeat''s worth of traffic. The question has anybody acknowledged this box has to survive registration, so it gets its own column and only moves one way. NULL is a real and expected state: an unconfigured Pi mints its own uid and comes up working. What it cannot do is claim to be an existing station: a minted uid is random, so it always makes its own row and can never take over another one.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN edge_registry.schema_version IS 'The applied schema_migrations version the edge''s register payload last reported (protocol.EdgeRegister.SchemaVersion; v137), written only from the wire, never by a human. NULL means no register has carried one.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN edge_registry.clock_offset_ms IS 'The edge''s clock minus Core''s, as its last heartbeat implied it: the envelope''s ts minus Core''s clock on receipt, the broker''s delay included (v154). Written by the heartbeat''s UPDATE; NULL until a heartbeat carried a ts.';
COMMENT ON INDEX edge_registry_station_uid_key IS 'Partial, because a row can exist with no uid yet: a heartbeat from a legacy Edge that has not been enrolled. A plain UNIQUE would collapse every such row onto one another under the empty string, many edges silently sharing one identity, the failure the identity change exists to end.';

COMMENT ON COLUMN node_properties.updated_at IS 'When the row last moved (v90). The property UPSERT changes the value in place, so a created_at-only row read as untouched forever. The audit_log says who and what; this says when.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN node_bin_types.created_at IS 'When the row was written (v90); the table carried no timestamp before. Written by the group settings modal, as node_properties is.';

COMMENT ON COLUMN cms_transactions.source_type IS 'No DEFAULT: every writer sets it, and a default that silently supplies it is a place for a future writer to forget. Historical rows carry correction and stay browsable.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN cms_transactions.delta IS 'Signed: negative leaves the boundary, positive arrives at it. There is deliberately no txn_type (it was sign(delta) as a word, free to disagree with the delta beside it) and no qty_before or qty_after (computed at insert from a recursive subtree scan, unsound under concurrent moves; v101 dropped them). A running total is a query, not a column.';

COMMENT ON TABLE mission_events IS 'Raw vendor observations, one row per transition: what the fleet reported, in what order, with the robot where. Fed by the RDS status poller, so new_state holds vendor states (RUNNING, WAITING, FINISHED: SEER''s vocabulary), not Core''s order statuses. The one exception is BLOCK_FINISHED, Core''s own per-leg marker, deliberately not a vendor state so a reader filtering on real states does not pick it up. Not the source for canonical timing: two clocks meet here, and order_history is the one written by Core''s own state machine. Same subject as mission_telemetry at a different grain, answering a different question: do not merge them, and do not grow this into a leg table beyond the per-block rows it carries.';
COMMENT ON TABLE mission_telemetry IS 'What the robots did, one row per mission: what the fleet actually carried out, not what happened to the orders. Written only when an order reaches a vendor terminal (finalizeMissionTelemetry, gated on fleet.IsTerminalState), so most terminal orders have no row here. That survivorship is correct for the question this table answers, since every skip and most cancels never reached a robot, and wrong for any question about order outcomes, which belong to orders and order_history. Reading failure counts from here was a real bug (see GetFailures).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN mission_telemetry.duration_ms IS 'Lead time (created to terminal, queue included). Execution time, what the robot actually spent with faulted dwell excluded, is computed on read from order_history (store/telemetry/execution.go).';

COMMENT ON COLUMN demand_registry.replenish_uop_threshold IS 'UOP-threshold replenishment: when above 0, Core''s threshold monitor holds this (loader, payload) loop''s combined in-loop UOP (bins and buckets) against it. 0 is off.';

COMMENT ON TABLE lineside_buckets IS 'Core''s mirror of the Edge''s bucket model: parts at a node, by payload. core_node_name is the canonical node name, not the Edge''s process_nodes.id: the two int64 namespaces share a type and mean different things at each end, and reading the Edge''s id as Core''s produced cross-plant attribution bugs. It resolves to nodes.id through GetNodeByName. The station left the key at v65: which node holds the parts is the fact, which Pi reported it is not. The key is (core_node_name, payload_code, state), state active or stranded (v131).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN lineside_buckets.station IS 'Attribute data, not identity: the station that last reported this pile. Never a predicate (v65).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN lineside_buckets.payload_code IS 'The payload this pile''s parts belong to, filled by the Edge at capture from the order context. Empty means unknown (an orphan whose claim was deleted before the capture resolved a payload) and is excluded from SystemUOPForPayload: a conservative undercount, never an overcount.';

COMMENT ON TABLE inventory_delta_dedup IS 'At-most-once dedup for inventory delta envelopes. Distinct from inbox dedup, which gates order-message processing: this gates the count change the envelope carries. last_seq is the highest SequenceID Core has applied for the (station, scope_kind, scope_key) tuple; epoch is the bin''s delta_epoch when the delta was emitted (bucket-scope deltas carry 0). A bucket''s row is not cleared when the pile empties, and must not be: since v126 (applied_net) an absent row applies the station''s whole running net. The admin delete resets the row instead of deleting it (store/inventory/inventory.go).';

COMMENT ON TABLE dashboards IS 'Floor-facing definitions for the wall-display platform. A row is a saved, named view of Core''s live data, scoped to a set of stations (an area), rendered chromeless at /wall-display/{id}?kiosk=1 and managed on the hub at /. Pure presentation: it owns no operational data beyond its own definition. kind selects the renderer (task-board, robot-map, heartbeat, node-report); stations_json is the area filter; config_json carries per-kind options. The table name matches the /api/.../dashboards namespace and domain.Dashboard, not the page''s name.';

COMMENT ON TABLE cell_part_events IS 'One row per PLC counter tick, projected from production.ticks (and production.tick from older Edges). Partitioned monthly by recorded_at: store/heartbeat creates the current and next month''s partitions at boot and drops partitions older than the retention window nightly. The dedup key is the unique index uq_cell_part_events_tick (v128). cell_id is the envelope''s station, the cell identity the /api/cells endpoints key on.';
COMMENT ON TABLE cell_targets IS 'Admin-editable target cycle time per cell and payload, for the live-state and loss-accounting math. Falls back to a rolling estimate when no row exists.';
COMMENT ON TABLE downtime_events IS 'Persisted downtime start and end events from the sim''s clustered-random downtime model: explicit start/end pairs for OEE availability, in place of gap analysis over cell_part_events. Partitioned monthly by started_at, as cell_part_events is. Dedup on (station, edge_event_id) through downtime_event_dedup.';

COMMENT ON TABLE schema_migrations IS 'The migration runner''s own bookkeeping (protocol/migrate): one row per applied version. A database born on the baseline records the whole retired chain.';
COMMENT ON TABLE bin_loader_home_bin_types IS 'What each loader window can physically take (v75).';
COMMENT ON TABLE bin_loader_quotas IS 'A loader''s declared carrier mix (v75).';
COMMENT ON TABLE cms_postings IS 'One row per POST to the CMS middleware (v102). Separate from cms_transactions because a transaction is a local fact (a bin crossed a boundary) and a posting is a claim about a remote system (whether it knows). Their lifecycles do not line up: one posting carries many transactions, and a posting can be retried while the transactions it carries never change.';
COMMENT ON TABLE carrier_families IS 'The physical cart a bin type is one setting of (v172).';
COMMENT ON TABLE kanban_families IS 'The pool of each carrier family a kanban owns (v172).';
COMMENT ON TABLE kanbans IS 'One row per kanban: its station and strict (v169).';
COMMENT ON TABLE kanban_levels IS 'How many empty carriers of each type a kanban is to hold, and the type''s own overflow (v90 as node_maintain_levels, renamed at v169). Separate from bin_loader_quotas on purpose: a quota is a preference bounded by never-2N, a level is the number Core keeps.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kanban_layer_levels IS 'Per kanban, carrier type and height in layers, how many carts the kanban wants standing that high (v161 as node_maintain_layer_levels, renamed at v169). A height of 1 or more is communication, summed against nothing; the row at 0 (v164) is tops and bottoms, a cap of its own beside the type''s stack level in kanban_levels.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kanban_supports IS 'Which process nodes a kanban serves (v90 as node_maintain_supports, renamed at v169): the resolved node set, because a claim is Edge-local and Core cannot read one.';
COMMENT ON TABLE kanban_sources IS 'The groups a kanban''s keeper may pull from, in rank order (v169).';
COMMENT ON TABLE lineside_drain_ledger IS 'One row per applied lineside drain (v120). A drain is consumption at a node from a pile, not a bin event, so bin_uop_ledger cannot take it (bin_id is NOT NULL), and the pile''s row deletes at qty 0; this is where the history survives.';
COMMENT ON TABLE order_intake_refusals IS 'The pair legs Core refused at intake (v115). A refused request leaves no order row, so the partner naming one could not tell refused from not-received-yet; the pair rule reads this when the partner''s row is absent.';
COMMENT ON TABLE parts IS 'A part under both of the names it is known by (v107): the part number CMS books against and the cat id a cell''s PLC declares. A table rather than two columns on payload_manifest because a cat id belongs to the part, not to the line that mentions it, and because a foreign key from the line is the one guard that cannot be forgotten at a door.';
COMMENT ON TABLE process_positions IS 'The seats a process binds at that no claim names (loader windows and homes, FG drains), mirrored from plant.claims for addressing only (v152).';
COMMENT ON TABLE robot_confidence_daily IS 'The permanent per-robot residual roll-up (v77).';
COMMENT ON TABLE robot_confidence_low IS 'The long-retention low-confidence forensic trail (v77).';
COMMENT ON TABLE robot_confidence_samples IS 'Raw localization-confidence samples, daily partitions (v77).';
COMMENT ON TABLE lane_confidence_daily IS 'The permanent per-lane roll-up (v80), in place of the per-segment one, whose rows were at the directed-segment grain, half a lane.';
COMMENT ON TABLE lane_robot_confidence_daily IS 'The permanent per-lane, per-robot grain the map filters by AMR (v83). lane_confidence_daily merges its robots irreversibly, so this carries the intersection.';
COMMENT ON TABLE scene_diffs IS 'One row per observed map edit (v81): the join that relates an RDS /scene edit to a robot .smap edit made in the same sitting.';
COMMENT ON TABLE scene_lane_versions IS 'Per-lane geometry versions (v81): what lane_confidence_daily.version_id points at.';
COMMENT ON TABLE scene_map_versions IS 'The archived .smap blob, gzipped, the scan cloud in its own column (v81).';
COMMENT ON TABLE scene_areas IS 'Declared map areas, temporally versioned, parsed from the robot .smap (v81).';
COMMENT ON TABLE scene_reflectors IS 'Reflector positions, temporally versioned; identity is the position (v81).';
COMMENT ON TABLE area_confidence_daily IS 'The per-zone roll-up (v82). One reading can be in several zones (SEER areas overlap), so its samples column does not sum to the plant total.';
COMMENT ON TABLE plant_claims_reports IS 'Per process, the station whose plant-claims report the mirror holds and the digest it carried (v143); read back onto each station''s heartbeat ack.';
COMMENT ON TABLE plant_confidence_daily IS 'The plant-day record for counts that have no lane to hang on: orphans, unkeyable, unversioned, unattributed (v82).';
COMMENT ON TABLE tte_samples IS 'The per-line time-to-empty the sourceability pass computes on every full pass (v118). Written only by the two-minute full recompute, self-pruning at 45 days.';
COMMENT ON TABLE bin_uop_exception IS 'The permanent exceptions ledger (v93): no retention, ever.';
COMMENT ON TABLE bin_uop_delta_daily IS 'The permanent daily roll-up of the raw delta stream (v94): growth accepted.';
COMMENT ON TABLE cleaning_mission_setup IS 'The one air-knife mission set up on the Robots page''s Cleaning tab (v158; floor data, never yaml); read by the cleaning loop every tick and by the tab.';
COMMENT ON TABLE cleaning_missions IS 'One row per pinned cleaning order sent to the fleet, the only record of it: no Core orders row is ever written (v158). Read by the cleaning loop (open rows, last dispatch per robot) and the Cleaning tab''s history.';
COMMENT ON TABLE cleaning_opt_outs IS 'A row per robot never to clean (v158); read by the cleaning loop''s guard and the robot pop-up''s switch.';
COMMENT ON TABLE robot_hours IS 'The 2-second robot poll kept as totals per robot per UTC hour, permanent (v158); read by the Robots page''s Fleet tab and the cleaning trial (robot-days, km).';
COMMENT ON TABLE robot_deviceconfig_pulls IS 'The Device Config tab''s pull history (v184): one append-only row per robot per pull. params is the flattened leaf set the matrix reads (a tab open never re-parses a robot''s whole model); config is the SANITIZED whole model: credential values are SHA-256 fingerprints before either column is written, because a robot''s live model carries a wifi password in the clear. model_md5 hashes the raw bytes at pull time, kept as provenance only: the fleet''s status envelope model_md5 matches no robot''s file, so it is not a staleness signal and nothing reads this column for one.';
COMMENT ON TABLE robot_alarm_onsets IS 'Alarm onsets per robot per hour per code, permanent (v158), past RDS''s five-week alarm retention; read by the Fleet tab and the cleaning trial.';
COMMENT ON TABLE robot_lidar_hours IS 'One laser frame per parked robot per hour reduced per lidar, 90-day retention (v158); read per robot against itself by the cleaning trial''s before/after time join.';
